{% capture filters %}
[
  {%- for item in data.filters -%}
    {% liquid
      assign first_param_type = item.params[0].types[0] | downcase | default: 'untyped'
      # `variable` is the language server's name for the "accepts anything" bucket, and it selects
      # filters with `syntax.startsWith(inputType)`. `PseudoType` has no `object` member, so an
      # `object | …` syntax would match no bucket and those filters would never be offered.
      case first_param_type
      when 'object'
        assign first_param_type = 'variable'
      endcase

      assign default_description = 'returns ' | append: item.returns[0].description
    %}
    {
      {%- comment -%}
        `platformOS` marks the platform's own filters and is ABSENT on Liquid's: the language server
        reads the KEY's presence to pick which reference page to link.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "category": {% if item.platformOS %}"platformOS"{% else %}"liquid"{% endif %},
      "deprecated": {{ item.deprecated | default: false | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Read through under the name they are published as; the `deprecated_*` fallback serves a
        filters.json from a platform version before the generator emitted `deprecation_*`.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecation_reason": {{ item.deprecation_reason | default: item.deprecated_reason | default: '' | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        The successor's registered NAME: platformos-check renames a deprecated filter to it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecation_replacement": {{ item.deprecation_replacement | default: item.deprecated_replacement | default: '' | json }},
      "description": {{ item.description | json }},
      "summary": {{ item.description | default: default_description | json }},
      "syntax": "{% print first_param_type %} | {% print item.name %}",
      {% if item.platformOS %}"platformOS": true,{% endif %}
      "name": {{ item.name | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        `{% if %}`, not `| default: '[]'`: an empty array is blank in Liquid, so `default:` would
        substitute the two-character STRING "[]" where an array belongs.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "aliases": {% if item.aliases %}{{ item.aliases | json }}{% else %}[]{% endif %},
      {%- comment -%}
        `FilterArity` refuses a call outside this range, so it is contract, not display. `max` is
        null for a variadic filter — "cannot refuse an argument", not "unknown".

        `{% if %}` rather than `default:`, because 0 is a legitimate `max` and truthy in Liquid
        while nil is not, so `default:` would turn a real 0 into null.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "arity": {
        "min": {{ item.arity.min | json }},
        "max": {% if item.arity.max %}{{ item.arity.max | json }}{% else %}null{% endif %}
      },
      {%- comment -%}
        Whether the `positional: false` entries below are ALL the named arguments the filter takes —
        the one field here a consumer may act on by REFUSING a template. Three states: absent means
        nobody has said, and is not a synonym for either answer.

        `{% if %}`, not `| default:`: Liquid's `default` substitutes for `false` as readily as for
        nil, which would publish every open filter as closed.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% if item.named_parameters_exhaustive == true %}"named_parameters_exhaustive": true,{% endif %}
      {% if item.named_parameters_exhaustive == false %}"named_parameters_exhaustive": false,{% endif %}
      "parameters": [
        {% for param in item.params %}
          {
            "description": {{ param.description | json }},
            "name": {{ param.name | json }},
            "required": {{ param.required | default: false | json }},
            "variadic": {{ param.variadic | default: false | json }},
            "default": {{ param.default | default: '' | json }},
            {%- comment -%}
              "pass this by position, not as `name: value`" — the language server completes and
              hovers the false ones as the filter's named arguments. True when absent, for a
              filters.json from before the platform emitted named arguments at all.

              NOT `| default: true`, which substitutes for `false` exactly as it does for nil and so
              turned every named argument back into a positional one.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            "positional": {% if param.positional == false %}false{% else %}true{% endif %},
            "types": {% if param.types %}{{ param.types | json }}{% else %}[]{% endif %}
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ],
      "return_type": [
        {% for return_type in item.returns %}
          {
            "type": "{% print return_type.types | join: ', ' | downcase %}",
            "name": "",
            "description": {{ return_type.description | json }},
            "array_value": ""
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ],
      "examples": [
        {% for example in item.examples %}
          {
            "name": "",
            "description": "",
            "syntax": "",
            "path": "",
            "raw_liquid": {{ example | json }},
            "parameter": false,
            "display_type": "text",
            "show_data_tab": true
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ]
    }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
  {% endfor %}
]
{% endcapture %}
{% liquid
  assign filters = filters | parse_json: raw_text: true

  return filters
%}
